  "notes": "RedZone's melody-and-drum-loop pop-R&B with The-Dream, 1999–2010. Tempo is \"87–100 straight (backbeat 2&4)\" and the mode is 87, the sourced reading. The board delta that matters most is his starting point: \"he builds from a **found drum loop** rather than programming from scratch\", the sourced record starting from GarageBand's Vintage Funk Kit 03 \"chosen for its 'walloping hi-hat'\". So the hat lane is the loudest element in this model — 0.74 fill density, a main-velocity band to 0.98, open hats on both offbeats, a ramped roll at 0.28 per bar — over a plain two-or-three-hit kick and a snare flat on 2 and 4 pushed 3–8 ms with a loose 0.7 quantize, because a sampled loop is not a grid. The enormous snare reverb has no schema field and is recorded here. Harmony follows the documented session: \"he plays chords under a topline in real time — 'Stewart put the next chord' while The-Dream sang; two-to-four-chord minor vamps with synth pads, no busy countermelody\" — hence `maxChords` 4, a minor family set, and a countermelody density of only 0.26 led by `sustain_pad`, unusually low for this section and authored *because* the research says there is no busy counterline. \"Melodies are built from consonant-heavy rhythmic cells\" is the staccato articulation, `twoBarCellRepeat` and a riff-dominant contour. The low end is \"distorted/overdriven bassline rather than clean sub — saturation is the identifying trait; bass locked to chord roots in whole or half notes\", so `followRootsProb` is 0.96 on single-degree cells with no `bass808` block. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every velocity and density number and `swing.amount` 0.55. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `Bbm`, `Gb`, `Dbm`, and the remaining value `Fm`, together with all 4 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
